@meta
id: 03-09
title: Scrap the line and type it again
chapter: 03
type: lesson
skills: insert-ctrl-w
par: 92
minutes: 5
vscode: triple-click the line, retype it
open: order.provider.ts

@brief
The day calculation is wrong twice over: it reads the wrong field, and it
divides milliseconds by a thousand, which gives seconds, not days. The
line should be:

    const days = (Date.now() - order.placedAt.getTime()) / 86_400_000;

Almost nothing of the original survives, so patching it word by word is
not worth it. Throw the line away and type it again — without leaving
insert mode, and without losing the indentation.

@keys
| `A` | append at the end of the line |
| `Ctrl-W` | delete the word before the cursor |
| `Ctrl-U` | delete everything before the cursor on this line |
| `Esc` | out |

@hints
`Ctrl-W` is the word-sized version. There is a line-sized one next to it
in the same family.
--
`Ctrl-U` clears the line back to the indent. Put the cursor at the end of
the line first, so "everything before the cursor" means the whole line.
--
Four `j` down to the line, `A` to get to the end of it in insert mode,
`Ctrl-U` to empty it, then type the replacement and `Esc`.

@solution
    j j j j A <Ctrl-U> const days = (Date.now() - order.placedAt.getTime()) / 86_400_000; <Esc> :wq<Enter>

Seventy-seven keystrokes, sixty-six of them the new line.

`A` then `Ctrl-U` is the pre-chapter-8 way to say "replace this line". It
keeps the indentation — `Ctrl-U` stops at the first non-blank, the same
place `I` starts from — so you retype the code and not the whitespace.
There is a proper normal-mode key for this, `cc`, and it arrives in
chapter 8. Until then this is the cheap version, and it is still worth
knowing afterwards for the times you are already typing.

The third key in that family is `Ctrl-R`. It pastes the contents of a
register without leaving insert mode, which is how you get a yanked line,
a filename or the last search back into your typing. Registers are
chapter 20's whole subject, so leave it alone for now — just file away
that insert mode can read from them.

Notice what all three have in common: they fix what you are *currently
typing*. That is the honest job of insert mode. Anything that is fixing
text that was already in the file wants a normal-mode command instead.

@start order.provider.ts
@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);
    const days = (Date.now() - order.createdAt.getTime()) / 1000;

    return days;
  }
}

@expected order.provider.ts
@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);
    const days = (Date.now() - order.placedAt.getTime()) / 86_400_000;

    return days;
  }
}
